\paragraph{$\pi_{0.5}$ random subsampling anomaly and RoPE sensitivity.}
Random selection on $\pi_{0.5}$ Object collapses from 74.0\% at $k{=}0.95$ to 9.0\% at $k{=}0.9$, then recovers to 74.0\% at $k{=}0.7$ (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). The re-indexing step of our pruning protocol (Section~\ref{sec:pruning_protocol}) offers a partial explanation. $\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector preserves a one-to-one mapping between spatial patches and token positions; when random removal shifts surviving tokens to new positions, the backbone's Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE)~\cite{su2024roformer} encode spatial content at positions the model never encountered during training, disrupting learned attention patterns. Supporting this hypothesis, X-VLA's BART encoder---which redistributes spatial information across tokens via cross-attention---shows no disruption peak at $k{=}0.85$: it degrades smoothly across three suites (60--68\%; Table~\ref{tab:xvla_k085_crosssuite}), while $\pi_{0.5}$ collapses to 3.6--9.2\% on Object at the same retention ratio. The V-shaped recovery at $k \leq 0.7$ remains unexplained.

