\begin{abstract}
Removing 5\% of vision tokens collapses SmolVLA from 59.2\% to 0.0\% success rate, while the same reduction on $\pi_{0.5}$ retains 98.2\% under importance-based selection but only 74.0\% under random selection. Token pruning transfers unpredictably across Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models because the vision connector governs token compressibility. We present an empirical study of this sensitivity along two axes: connector coupling (token-independent vs.\ token-entangling) and task complexity (single-step vs.\ multi-step manipulation). Token-entangling connectors exhibit task-amplified collapse: under random pruning at 90\% retention, X-VLA loses 1.4 percentage points on single-step tasks but 27.8 on multi-step tasks, a $20{\times}$ amplification. Scoring effectiveness exhibits a double dissociation: attention-based scoring is effective on entangling connectors ($+$31 percentage points over random) but is ineffective to harmful on token-independent ones ($-$2 to $-$30\,pp), while L2 norm is effective on token-independent connectors with a $k$-dependent margin ($+$35.6\,pp at 85\% retention decaying to $+$5.6\,pp at 95\%) but anti-informative on entangling ones ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp). The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms provides a coarse compressibility indicator, partitioning models into uniform, graded, and catastrophic classes without downstream evaluation. For pixel-shuffle connectors where all hard deletion strategies yield near-zero SR (${\leq}$6.6\%), learned re-encoding recovers over 60\% success rate at 75\% compression regardless of module architecture, establishing deletion as the failure mechanism. Evaluation spans six VLA architectures (450M to 7B), three connector families, and four LIBERO task suites.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models consume up to hundreds of vision tokens per frame, creating a computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control. Token pruning offers a training-free solution, but transferability across architectures cannot be assumed: removing just 5\% of vision tokens collapses one VLA to near-zero success while another retains full performance under importance-based selection. We conduct a systematic study across six VLA architectures (450M--7B parameters), three vision connector families, and four LIBERO task suites of increasing manipulation complexity. Two axes govern the pruning response. First, the vision connector determines compressibility type: token-independent connectors degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors exhibit threshold cliffs or complete collapse, with task complexity amplifying losses by up to $20{\times}$ on multi-step tasks. Second, scoring method effectiveness dissociates with connector type: attention-based and norm-based scoring each help on one connector family but hurt on the other, precluding a universal pruning recipe. The coefficient of variation of post-connector token norms provides a single-pass diagnostic that predicts these failure modes without downstream evaluation. For architectures where all deletion strategies fail, learned re-encoding recovers functional performance at 75\% compression, establishing deletion itself as the failure mechanism.
\end{abstract}

